WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.802 --> 00:00:07.260
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Hello, my
name is Peter-Mark Verwoerd.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.090
I'm the head of
migration architecture

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.590
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.600
And what I'd like to
do today is talk to you

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.540
a little bit about ways you
can accelerate your migration

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.180
towards the cloud.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.180
And what I'd like to
do is just to start off

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.490
by explaining how we
view IT applications

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.160
and where they
reside essentially

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.630
in the world of migration.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.470
And so if you look
at this chart,

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.510
you can see that all
applications really

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.360
fall very broadly into these
four quadrants, whether they're

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.960
on the cloud or not, and
greater or lesser degrees

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:40.990
of modernization.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:44.890
And so when we think about
where customers are going

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.370
and where customers would want
to be from the point of view

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.190
of the cloud
provider, we're hoping

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.740
that everyone would like
to be in that top quadrant

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.544
with the highlighted green area.

00:00:55.544 --> 00:00:57.210
And we really think
that that's actually

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.680
the best location
for applications

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.388
to go-- where they're
the most efficient,

00:01:00.388 --> 00:01:04.620
where they're secure, where
they have cost certainty

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.250
and cost efficiency.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.250
And so we really do believe
that that's the best

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.120
place for applications to go.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.940
However, we recognize
that, in reality,

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.420
most applications
actually reside

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.360
in that bottom left
quadrant where they still

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.720
sit inside of data centers.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.030
They maybe haven't
been modernized.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.210
They're using older
architectural patterns.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.094
And they aren't,
say, de-coupled.

00:01:26.094 --> 00:01:28.260
They aren't highly efficient,
and not containerized,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.160
and so on.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.100
And in reality, most
customer applications

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.680
actually do sit there today.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.810
And so as a cloud provider,
what we have to do

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.109
is really come to where
customers are today.

00:01:40.109 --> 00:01:42.150
And that's something that
Google Cloud has really

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.190
strived to do in the
last couple of years--

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:47.700
is not just, say, here's
this lofty goal of cloud

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.240
and we hope you guys get there.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.960
But that actually we're
going to be providing tools,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.390
and methods, and
best practices to be

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.330
able to go from this bottom
left to the top right.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.210
And if you look at this
chart, really there

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.620
are essentially two
paths from the bottom

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.890
left to the top right.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.937
Either you can move over,
and modernize applications,

00:02:05.937 --> 00:02:07.770
and refactor them, and
so on, and leave them

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.380
inside the center, and
then move up to the cloud.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.260
Or you can move up
from the data center

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.010
into the cloud with relatively
static applications that

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.000
don't change too much
in that transition.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.620
And then slowly transition
them over to the right side

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.020
as you modernize, and refactor,
and rewrite applications.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:30.150
And so we feel that both
of these routes are valid

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:35.460
and both of them have paths
of success that you could do.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.860
However, today what
we're going to do

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.930
is focus on the path where
you go up and over first.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:46.950
Because the reality is customers
and customer applications

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.170
are actually really
everywhere on this quadrant.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.290
Very few people are sitting
in just one quadrant.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.780
People have applications
in data centers,

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.342
moved applications out of
data centers into the cloud,

00:02:57.342 --> 00:02:58.800
and moved them over
into the cloud.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.508
They've modernized
inside the data center

00:03:00.508 --> 00:03:03.090
and there's, generally
speaking, a very wide spread

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:04.260
of applications everywhere.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.770
And so there are many, many
paths of success in migration.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.190
And we're just going to
focus on, just for today,

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.870
on moving those applications
that sit in data centers

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:17.560
and moving them up into
the cloud as they are.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.710
And so kind of an open
question to this audience here.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:23.950
If you've ever worked on
a migration to the cloud

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:27.100
from, say, a data center,
what kind of challenges

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.720
did you have?

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:29.960
I'm sure there were many.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.400
And so I, along with many
others here at Google Cloud who

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.610
have worked on other migrations,
sat down and brainstormed,

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.100
what were the top 10
challenges that we came across.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:44.660
And we had a fairly
obvious trend that came up,

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:48.710
in that they were really cut
in half by technical challenges

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:51.510
and business challenges--

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.270
were really things that slowed
down acceleration and lowered

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:56.270
our chance of success.

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:59.470
So if we go through these
and we look at these business

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:02.770
challenges, these mostly
have to do with people,

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.570
and getting the right
people, and having

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:09.100
the right people biting into the
idea of a migration and really

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.590
see that it's going to be
successful for the company.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.048
And these are the
kinds of problems

00:04:13.048 --> 00:04:15.160
that really can't
be solved by running

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.784
a script, or an application,
or deploying some new version

00:04:17.784 --> 00:04:18.700
of something or other.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.519
This is really about
people believing

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:24.040
that the migration is the
best thing for the company

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.170
and that Google Cloud
is the best place to go.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.900
And we've encountered
these sort of situations.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:34.090
But these situations
that you encounter,

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.370
they really can slow
down your migration.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.640
So if you look at things
like, in particular,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:43.300
these first two bullets where
maybe management doesn't

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.910
believe 100% in the migration.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.280
That can really impede
a lot of progress.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.620
Or if people are
expecting the moon

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.320
and really you're
going into low orbit,

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:56.110
for example, that
can really slow down

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:57.670
the pace of the migration.

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:00.310
Or, in fact, when
different groups

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:01.840
have different ideas
of what exactly

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.506
the migration is
supposed to accomplish.

00:05:03.506 --> 00:05:07.630
And so being clear about
what you're trying to do,

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:10.120
and who needs to be involved,
and who needs to support this

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.030
is so important for having
a successful migration.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:16.130
And this is the kind of
thing that, as a provider,

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:19.630
we can do quite a bit to
help the people convince

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:20.680
senior management.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.740
We can work with
different groups

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.742
across your company
to be able to describe

00:05:24.742 --> 00:05:26.200
the benefits of
what a migration is

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.610
and what Google Cloud can do.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:29.068
And there are also
several programs

00:05:29.068 --> 00:05:31.210
that we offer from
different groups

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:34.300
within our company that actually
try to address these situations

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.604
upfront in a programmatic way.

00:05:35.604 --> 00:05:38.020
But at the end of the day,
most of the business challenges

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:41.700
are really about people
rather than technical issues.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.119
On the other hand, there
are always technical issues.

00:05:44.119 --> 00:05:45.660
With any technical
project, of course

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.285
there are going to
be technical issues.

00:05:47.285 --> 00:05:50.359
And we encounter these
frequently as well.

00:05:50.359 --> 00:05:52.650
But it's important to recognize
them for what they are,

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:56.670
so you can start preventing
them from happening if you can

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:58.650
as you start on your
migration project,

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.830
so that you don't encounter
the issues that we've had.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.119
Where we have had
migration projects really

00:06:04.119 --> 00:06:05.910
slow down because some
things that maybe we

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:07.390
didn't foresee at the time.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:13.010
So things like automation
really speed up migration

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:14.790
the more that you
invest in automation.

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:16.830
And so we've noticed
that across customers

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:20.070
that we've encountered, where
the more automated customers

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.496
are already, and the more of an
automation culture they have,

00:06:23.496 --> 00:06:24.870
the easier the
migration becomes.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.690
And the more that you can
do that before you migrate,

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:30.270
the easier your
migration will become.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.520
And I think this second point
is actually probably one

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:33.644
of the more important ones.

00:06:33.644 --> 00:06:36.439
We often get, say, the
opposite reaction of people

00:06:36.439 --> 00:06:37.480
who are not enthusiastic.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.021
We get people who are extremely
enthusiastic about migrating,

00:06:40.021 --> 00:06:43.260
which is fantastic, but then
you come into a situation

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.350
where they want to do absolutely
everything all at once

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:51.192
and you get a bit of a
feature creep situation

00:06:51.192 --> 00:06:52.650
where people just
want to add more,

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.220
and more, and more
to this product,

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:55.275
and it becomes untenable.

00:06:55.275 --> 00:06:56.910
It becomes undoable.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.590
And then instead
of actually doing

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:00.585
this grand ambitious
effort, actually it

00:07:00.585 --> 00:07:03.330
gets all pulled back
into, in the worst case

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:04.690
scenario, nothing at all.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.620
And so there has to
be some sort of limits

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.410
on what is realistic to do
in a particular time frame

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:13.840
and what needs to
be accomplished.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:17.710
We've gone into customers that,
for example, had, say, a very

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:19.615
large MySQL back end database.

00:07:19.615 --> 00:07:21.490
They were really
enthusiastic about, not only

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:24.052
moving to the cloud, but,
say, let's reconsider MySQL.

00:07:24.052 --> 00:07:26.260
Maybe we should move to a
different kind of database.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:29.230
Maybe we should move to managed
databases, like Cloud SQL.

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:31.240
Or we should try
a NoSQL database.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:33.270
Maybe we could shard
out the data structure

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:34.722
into different
kinds of databases.

00:07:34.722 --> 00:07:36.430
We had to caution them
against doing that

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:37.305
during the migration.

00:07:37.305 --> 00:07:40.120
We said, look, we have
a specific timeline

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.760
to accomplish what you
need to accomplish here.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.242
Let's get through that.

00:07:44.242 --> 00:07:45.700
And then after
you're migrated, you

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.070
can reconsider
your design options

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:52.559
and look at your database
in a calmer atmosphere.

00:07:52.559 --> 00:07:54.100
And once they did
that, they actually

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:55.414
saw the benefit of doing that.

00:07:55.414 --> 00:07:57.580
And once the migration was
finished and was finished

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.470
successfully, then
they took the time

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.930
to actually examine
what were their database

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:04.270
needs in a way that didn't
impact the migration.

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:06.880
And they actually had a much
more reasoned conversation

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:08.440
among themselves
about how to migrate

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:10.090
that particular component.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.460
And so it's really
important, if you've

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:15.700
got a big enthusiasm build
up, which is fantastic,

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.510
not to get too carried away.

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:19.420
And so these
technical challenges

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:21.724
are things that
we try to address

00:08:21.724 --> 00:08:23.140
with technical
solutions, but they

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.389
are things that you want to
watch out for because they

00:08:25.389 --> 00:08:29.650
really do slow down migrations.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:31.720
And so when we look
at what slows down

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:34.390
or decelerates
migrations, we also

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:37.299
want to talk to you about what
would accelerate a migration.

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:40.299
And we think this is where
our best practices really

00:08:40.299 --> 00:08:41.690
come into play.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.900
And this is really what
we want to focus on

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:45.220
for the rest of this talk--

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.980
about what accelerates
your migration,

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:51.200
what moves it forward,
about what can help you.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:52.960
And so these are best
practices, again,

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.330
gathered from
different coworkers

00:08:55.330 --> 00:09:00.120
that I have and myself on
the actual best practices

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.460
that we have learned from
working with customers.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:05.580
So this is something that
is practical experience

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:09.277
that we have seen actually
doing the act of the migrating.

00:09:09.277 --> 00:09:10.860
And so I think the
most important one,

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:12.690
and this is what we're going
to spend most of the time on,

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:14.324
is building a proper
cloud foundation.

00:09:14.324 --> 00:09:15.990
And we're going to
go into that in quite

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:19.879
a bit more detail, so I'm
going to leave that for now.

00:09:19.879 --> 00:09:21.670
While you're building
your cloud foundation

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.390
and throughout your migration,
the next most important thing

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:27.675
to do is actually think about
security the whole time.

00:09:27.675 --> 00:09:29.550
So this is not something
that you think about

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:30.080
at the beginning.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.121
This is not something you
think about at the end.

00:09:32.121 --> 00:09:34.710
This is part of every
single step of a migration.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.760
Otherwise it's really
just not going to work.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:37.725
You're going to have
either people complaining,

00:09:37.725 --> 00:09:40.260
or you're going to have
things that are not secure,

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:42.589
or, worst of both worlds,
you're going to have both.

00:09:42.589 --> 00:09:44.880
And so it's very important
to make that a consideration

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.920
while you do your entire
migration the whole time.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.120
As I said before, automation
can really improve a migration.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:56.870
The more that you can do it,
the faster and easier it can be.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:00.680
It's the difference between
having to manually copy, say,

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:03.320
an entire application or
maybe an entire operating

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:06.140
system from one
location to another,

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:09.315
verifying that the copy happened
correctly, copying over data,

00:10:09.315 --> 00:10:11.690
making sure the tests run,
making sure the application is

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:12.806
connected the same way.

00:10:12.806 --> 00:10:14.180
Versus just saying,
no, I'm going

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:16.909
to redeploy this application
from one location to another.

00:10:16.909 --> 00:10:18.950
You're talking about the
difference between hours

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:21.437
versus minutes, or even
days versus minutes,

00:10:21.437 --> 00:10:22.520
the more you can automate.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:24.186
And so everything you
can do to automate

00:10:24.186 --> 00:10:26.644
your infrastructure
in your environments

00:10:26.644 --> 00:10:27.560
is only going to help.

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:34.010
Fourth, the more you
can prepare for change

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:36.650
and the more you can
slice up your migration

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:40.050
into manageable chunks,
the easier it will be.

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:43.310
So if you can build iteratively
on your migration plan,

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:47.000
if you can say these steps
are going to happen, and then

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.880
build on those steps, and
just be prepared for one step

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:52.430
to influence another step in
a way that you didn't expect,

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:55.850
you're going to be much better
suited and better prepared

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:57.152
for success.

00:10:57.152 --> 00:10:58.860
Rather than having
one, two, three steps,

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:02.450
maybe have like one through 20,
one through 50, whatever it is,

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:03.800
smaller, more manageable steps.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:06.530
And that way you can adjust
as you go along, as you're

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:07.460
making this migration.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:09.418
Perhaps this is the first
time you've done this

00:11:09.418 --> 00:11:11.660
and you really need to
watch what you're doing,

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:14.630
and how you're getting
there, and how it's moving,

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:17.450
so you really don't want to
be too rigid in your plan.

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:21.780
So be prepared for change and
it will work out pretty well.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:24.110
And then finally,
once you're migrated,

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:25.130
you're not really done.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:28.117
That's just the first step of
actually existing on the cloud

00:11:28.117 --> 00:11:29.450
and having an application there.

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:31.970
So really you need to prepare
for what we call day two.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:33.830
So day one is this migration.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:37.130
Day two is this application now
exists in a new environment.

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:38.090
It needs to be managed.

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:39.050
You need to monitor it.

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:40.340
You need to log it.

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:43.920
You need to audit it, redeploy
new versions, and so on.

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:45.740
So you need to prepare
to manually operate

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:46.610
this-- not manually.

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:49.970
But you need to actually
operate this application

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:51.140
as it exists in the cloud.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:54.480
So you need to prepare
for that as you migrate.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.010
Now, I said I was
going to go back

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:58.870
to what a cloud foundation
is and how important this is

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:00.120
in terms of the best practice.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:03.424
And so we're going to dive
into that a little bit more.

00:12:03.424 --> 00:12:04.840
And so creating a
cloud foundation

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:07.085
is really about creating
the location where

00:12:07.085 --> 00:12:09.460
your applications are going
to migrate before you migrate

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:10.960
a single application.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:14.080
And the reason this
is so important

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:17.740
is that, if you can
properly migrate

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:21.520
into an already created, an
already vetted, an already

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:25.120
secure, or already properly
built out foundation,

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:27.700
it's going to make the
migration significantly easier.

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:30.492
Rather than trying to do that as
you migrate your applications,

00:12:30.492 --> 00:12:32.950
very much kind of like trying
to build a plane while you're

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:33.950
trying to fly the plane.

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:36.370
You really want to have
this foundation set

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:38.560
before the first
migration happens.

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:40.406
And then once that
migration happens,

00:12:40.406 --> 00:12:42.280
you don't have to think
about all these parts

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:44.177
of the foundation that exist.

00:12:44.177 --> 00:12:46.510
You can just keep on migrating
applications and learning

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:48.599
what's unique about
your application,

00:12:48.599 --> 00:12:50.140
about your company,
about your setup,

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:52.807
rather than having to figure out
what is unique about this cloud

00:12:52.807 --> 00:12:54.223
environment that
you're moving to,

00:12:54.223 --> 00:12:56.800
which is really what you're
figuring out when you establish

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.400
your cloud foundation.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:02.580
So if we look at
the first one, this

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.040
is not, say, rocket science.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:07.530
This kind of makes sense that
the first thing you should do

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:10.970
is pick the first application
that you want to move.

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:13.480
But picking that application
can be very difficult

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:15.810
and it can be non-intuitive,
or maybe perhaps

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:18.270
not even obvious if you
haven't done this before.

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:21.360
So the characteristics
of, say, a good mover

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:24.810
is something that is not going
to bring the business down

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:26.080
if it doesn't work.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:27.840
On the other hand,
it can't be so far

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:29.880
out of the relevancy
of the business

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:32.110
that it's not going to be
important if it does move.

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:35.460
So you want things
that are still

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:37.830
a working application of
business application, something

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:38.371
that matters.

00:13:38.371 --> 00:13:39.810
It's not a POC.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:43.500
It's not just a
throwaway application.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:45.150
It is something
real, but it is not

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:48.990
something that is going
to break your business.

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.490
But you still have
to learn from it.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:55.450
You still have to have some sort
of knowledge gained from it.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:57.600
And also, you have to
be able to control it.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:59.550
So if it's a license
that you don't control,

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:01.740
if there's particular
hardware or software that

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.340
connects to it that
you can't control,

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:04.714
that's not something
that's going

00:14:04.714 --> 00:14:05.979
to make a good first mover.

00:14:05.979 --> 00:14:08.020
And if you look at the
technical characteristics,

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:09.504
they're similar in theme.

00:14:09.504 --> 00:14:10.920
You don't want
something that's so

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:13.520
tied to the rest of the business
from a technical standpoint

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:15.145
that it's going to
be hard to untangle.

00:14:15.145 --> 00:14:16.890
You don't want
something that's so

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:18.402
intertwined with
software licenses,

00:14:18.402 --> 00:14:19.860
it's going to be
difficult to move.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:22.260
You want something that
can cleanly move on its own

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.770
as easily as
possible, rather than

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:27.840
something that's
really wound up inside

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:31.250
of many, many other things.

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:34.350
And so if we look at, say,
an example application

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:37.380
where we have users and
external applications connecting

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:39.740
to a website and a
mobile application.

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:41.600
And there's a log in system.

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:43.770
There's blog posts, and so on.

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:49.290
Some sort of, say, customer
commentary system, for example.

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:50.612
It could be a lot of things.

00:14:50.612 --> 00:14:52.320
And if we look at this
whole system, what

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:53.920
would be a good first mover?

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:56.100
For example, this
mobile application

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:58.800
doesn't really depend on much
of the rest of the application

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:00.090
stack.

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:02.650
It's got pretty simple
inputs and outputs

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:05.610
and it can be pulled out and
put in a different application

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:07.680
location fairly easily.

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:14.300
And so this would make a pretty
good example of a first mover.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:16.582
On the other hand,
bad first movers

00:15:16.582 --> 00:15:18.540
are pretty much the
opposite of everything else

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:20.081
we suggested-- things
that are deeply

00:15:20.081 --> 00:15:22.280
entangled with the rest
of the application stack;

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:25.610
licenses that don't lend
themselves to being migrated;

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:28.659
things that are either far too
business critical to move first

00:15:28.659 --> 00:15:30.950
or things that are so irrelevant
that they don't really

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:33.350
prove anything; edge
cases that are, again,

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.600
so far out there that they're
not going to be relevant

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:38.390
for the rest of your
application base

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:41.450
don't particularly
make good first movers.

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:44.300
And so if we look, again, at
the same application stack,

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:48.440
this blog post pool that sits
in the middle of everything

00:15:48.440 --> 00:15:50.990
would be really
difficult to move first.

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:53.160
It's got so many connections
to everything else,

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:56.540
so many dependencies on data
in upstream and downstream

00:15:56.540 --> 00:15:59.949
systems, it would just be
so much more challenging

00:15:59.949 --> 00:16:01.490
than picking a
different application.

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.490
So it would be a much
more difficult application

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:06.840
to move first.

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:10.320
Next, you want to establish
an identity on the cloud.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.090
So people are going
to be using your cloud

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:15.780
environment-- your users, your
developers, operations, and so

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:16.509
on.

00:16:16.509 --> 00:16:18.300
You need to establish
who those people are,

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.130
and how they are
going to get access,

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:22.920
and what those
identities actually mean.

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:25.000
And as far as Google
Cloud is concerned,

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:27.447
we have different identities,
which are either people,

00:16:27.447 --> 00:16:29.280
they're groups, or
they're service accounts,

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:33.090
so they are automated systems
acting in certain ways.

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:38.020
And they would become roles.

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:41.250
And those roles have permission
to act on certain services.

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:44.100
And so you've got a great deal
of granularity and flexibility

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:45.990
in how you want to
set that up in order

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:49.480
to do pretty much anything
you need to do in the cloud.

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:51.510
And so if you look at
the types of identities

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:52.885
that we have in
the cloud, you've

00:16:52.885 --> 00:16:56.580
got actual people,
human accounts,

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:58.560
versus service accounts,
which are not human.

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:01.304
Those are robotic accounts,
automated accounts,

00:17:01.304 --> 00:17:02.970
however you would
like to describe them.

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:05.160
You have groups and
then, finally, domains.

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:06.839
And these are all
the different ways

00:17:06.839 --> 00:17:08.589
that identity comes
together in the cloud.

00:17:08.589 --> 00:17:10.089
And the really key
thing here that I

00:17:10.089 --> 00:17:11.760
would like you to
take away from this

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.190
is that there are a couple
of ways of setting this up.

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:16.167
And we try to be as
flexible as we can in order

00:17:16.167 --> 00:17:17.250
to meet you where you are.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:19.083
If you don't have a
current identity system,

00:17:19.083 --> 00:17:20.760
then you can set
that up in the cloud

00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:22.589
and set that up
with Google Cloud.

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:24.240
Or if you already
have an existing one,

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.115
then you can synchronize
that with the cloud.

00:17:26.115 --> 00:17:29.430
And so that maintains your
existing identity structure.

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:32.000
But the point is that we want
to meet you with what you need

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:33.000
and what you have today.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:39.610
Third, organizing your
resources is critical.

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:42.560
So once you have decided
what is going to move,

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:45.760
you've decided who
are your people

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:48.280
and what are their roles
going to be in the cloud,

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:50.140
deciding how you're
going to organize

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:51.490
your cloud is very important.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:54.049
In Google Cloud, you can have
an organization with a number

00:17:54.049 --> 00:17:55.090
of folders underneath it.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:57.142
Within these folders,
you can add projects.

00:17:57.142 --> 00:17:59.350
And the projects actually
hold the resources-- things

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:02.990
like virtual machines,
databases, queues, and so on.

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:07.300
And so there's a layering of
organization that you can add.

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:09.430
And how you decide
to do this really

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:11.980
depends on your
organizational preferences.

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:14.825
I mean, we have a number
of ideas and suggestions,

00:18:14.825 --> 00:18:16.450
but it's really going
to depend on what

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.380
suits your company best.

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:20.380
So if we take a look at,
say, maybe the simplest

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:23.950
way of organizing a company,
you have an organization

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:24.800
at the top.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:26.800
Then you have folders for
environments, and then

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:28.330
applications underneath.

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:30.950
The great thing about this
is it's fairly simple.

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:31.975
It's intuitive.

00:18:31.975 --> 00:18:35.230
It does not get
complicated very soon.

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:39.160
On the downside,
this is not something

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:41.590
that lends itself to flexibility
if you have environments

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:42.900
that don't quite fit in here.

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:44.320
If you have things
like shared services,

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:46.320
you have no knowledge of
the different divisions

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:47.660
within a company.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.200
So if you take a slightly
more complicated view,

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:54.899
you could say, let's organize
at a second level by function.

00:18:54.899 --> 00:18:57.190
So you have applications,
and you have shared services.

00:18:57.190 --> 00:18:59.600
and then underneath, a
relatively similar structure.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:01.150
And so the downside
is you're getting

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:02.680
a little more complicated.

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:05.050
The upside is you've got
a little more flexibility

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:06.670
in terms of adding
shared services,

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:09.430
recognizing that there
are different functions

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:12.679
of applications, and
so you can add that.

00:19:12.679 --> 00:19:14.470
And then finally, if
we look at, say, maybe

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:18.370
the most complex recommendation
that we start with,

00:19:18.370 --> 00:19:20.050
you're actually
dividing up applications

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:23.500
by division before you get down
to function or environment.

00:19:23.500 --> 00:19:26.480
And the upside of this is you
get fantastic flexibility.

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:29.530
You can really define and
describe your organization

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:31.030
in Google Cloud
the way it actually

00:19:31.030 --> 00:19:32.530
is reflected in reality.

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:35.270
The downside is that it is much
more complicated to manage.

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:39.280
It's much more complicated even
to visualize and conceptualize.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:41.399
And crossing different
project barriers

00:19:41.399 --> 00:19:43.190
and different folder
barriers may, in fact,

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:44.841
be more difficult.
But it's all going

00:19:44.841 --> 00:19:46.840
to depend on the plus and
minus of what you want

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:48.400
and what works best for you.

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:50.316
And so there are different
ways of doing that.

00:19:50.316 --> 00:19:52.390
We're capable of supporting
whatever those are,

00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:55.880
but it's important for you to
decide what works best for you.

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:05.210
Next, once you have this
foundation created--

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:09.760
so you've selected your
first mover already,

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:11.344
you've decided who
your people are

00:20:11.344 --> 00:20:13.260
going to be when they're
in the cloud, decided

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:15.460
what that organization
is going to look like.

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:18.610
And then next, you actually
need to group those individuals

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:20.650
and resources
together so that you

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:24.310
can figure out who's going to
do what, when, how, to whomever.

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:29.950
So this is an example
grouping that we

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:33.070
could recommend for groups
starting out in the cloud.

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:34.690
In the first one,
you want to have

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:36.910
a group that's going
to define and control

00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:40.031
how your organization
is defined,

00:20:40.031 --> 00:20:42.280
and what permissions you're
going to give to everyone,

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:44.470
where is everyone going to go?

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:46.570
At the top level,
what are the things

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:48.370
that you're going
to decide to do?

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:53.170
And so this group is only
going to decide what things are

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:57.280
and what things can be
within the organization.

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:00.580
Ideally, they're going to
decide very little else.

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:02.740
So you want to have
separation of duties

00:21:02.740 --> 00:21:04.714
as clearly as possible.

00:21:04.714 --> 00:21:06.880
Next, you could have a
network administration group.

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:09.432
And this group,
as you may assume,

00:21:09.432 --> 00:21:11.140
is going to define
how the network works.

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:12.530
They're going to define
what kind of networks

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:14.980
you're going to use, where
are the firewalls, who's

00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:16.330
going to have access
to what, how are you

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:18.538
going to make connections
between different projects,

00:21:18.538 --> 00:21:21.346
different networks, cloud
to data center, and so on.

00:21:21.346 --> 00:21:22.720
And they're going
to decide that.

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:23.650
And once again,
you're going to want

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:25.810
to have a narrow focus
on that, so that is all

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:28.540
that that group is
responsible for.

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:30.370
Same idea with
security, where you're

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:34.420
going to have security
administrator group define what

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:36.440
is permissible for the
different roles, what

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:38.440
is permissible with the
different networks, what

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:41.320
is permissible, say,
from a virtual machine.

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:44.350
And that that is the sole focus
of what they are going to do.

00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:47.635
And then finally, actually
a very common grouping,

00:21:47.635 --> 00:21:48.970
is actually billing.

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:51.640
So people who are responsible
for how the company gets

00:21:51.640 --> 00:21:55.180
billed, how the individual
divisions within the company

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:58.090
are going to get chargeback
based on the total cloud bill

00:21:58.090 --> 00:21:58.952
and so on.

00:21:58.952 --> 00:22:00.160
And that is their sole focus.

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:03.294
And that is a set of permissions
that you can sequester off

00:22:03.294 --> 00:22:04.960
from everything else,
where they're only

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:07.460
looking at how the bills come
in, where they're coming from,

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:10.360
who is charging what, and
who is spending so much.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.770
And these are
really just getting

00:22:12.770 --> 00:22:14.060
started recommendations.

00:22:14.060 --> 00:22:16.730
There is a lot more flexibility
and a lot more granularity

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:18.890
that you could add
over time, but these

00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:23.260
are strong recommendations
that we have upfront.

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:27.400
And then finally, the last step
of building out this foundation

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:28.870
is really defining your network.

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:32.782
So you have a first mover, you
have people and identities,

00:22:32.782 --> 00:22:34.240
you have an
organization structure,

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:37.450
you have groupings for
permissions, and roles,

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:38.870
and responsibilities.

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:40.600
And finally, you
need to really define

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:43.000
the network that these
applications are going

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:45.430
to live in, and what
your network wants to be

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:47.380
and what you need it
to be for your company

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:48.921
and for your
applications to succeed.

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:52.990
So the first thing
you need to decide

00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:54.800
is how you're going
to connect to Google.

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:56.780
So if you have existing
infrastructure already,

00:22:56.780 --> 00:22:58.640
you're going to have
to pick some kind

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:02.140
of connectivity option
upfront, whether that's

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:04.330
going over the public
internet, whether that's going

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:08.170
over carrier peering,
so partnering with some

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:12.790
of our partners to be able
to lease lines over carriers,

00:23:12.790 --> 00:23:15.554
or directly peering
with Google ourselves.

00:23:15.554 --> 00:23:17.470
And so these options are
all available to you.

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:23.500
They have greater or lesser
degrees of cost and speed,

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:25.060
so it really depends
on what you need

00:23:25.060 --> 00:23:26.380
and how long you need it for.

00:23:26.380 --> 00:23:28.600
None of these options
have to last forever.

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:30.880
So something you
could start with going

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:33.040
over the public
internet, creating a VPN.

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:35.860
Or you can move to direct
peering over time and then

00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:36.964
scale that back down.

00:23:36.964 --> 00:23:38.380
It really depends
on what you need

00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:39.747
and how long you need it for.

00:23:39.747 --> 00:23:41.830
So if you look at some of
these different options.

00:23:41.830 --> 00:23:44.240
If you look at, say, going
over the public internet

00:23:44.240 --> 00:23:49.000
and peering at an ISP, if you
look on the left hand side

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:55.390
here, you've got an
SLA between yourself

00:23:55.390 --> 00:23:57.310
and the peering
equipment at the ISP.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:23:59.230
And you've got
capacity management

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:02.350
between the ISP and the
Google peering location.

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:04.380
Meanwhile, if you
do direct peering,

00:24:04.380 --> 00:24:07.780
you're actually directly peering
with Google at an exchange

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:10.375
room in a data center,
or something like that,

00:24:10.375 --> 00:24:12.160
in the peering location with us.

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:16.330
And so you're cutting out
actually a lot more steps

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:17.140
in between.

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:20.140
And you've got more
direct connectivity.

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:22.450
You can pay for
higher bandwidth to be

00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:23.710
able to enable that as well.

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:29.311
And once you've got
connectivity to Google,

00:24:29.311 --> 00:24:31.310
you've got to decide,
what is your network going

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:32.600
to look like?

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:34.240
What regions are
you going to use?

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:37.324
Are you going to be
localized to, say, a region

00:24:37.324 --> 00:24:38.240
that's closest to you?

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:39.823
Are you going to use
multiple regions?

00:24:39.823 --> 00:24:42.230
Are you going to
use all regions?

00:24:42.230 --> 00:24:44.720
Google networks span
all regions by default.

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:47.600
And so you've got to decide,
in terms of operations,

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:50.420
where are you going to choose
to deploy applications,

00:24:50.420 --> 00:24:53.960
or where are you going to choose
to deploy virtual machines?

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:56.442
And how do you connect
these different networks,

00:24:56.442 --> 00:24:57.650
if you have multiple network?

00:24:57.650 --> 00:25:02.240
So the strategy we've seen for
many, many years has been a hub

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:05.930
and spoke, where at the hub
you've got shared services,

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:09.279
and in the spokes you've got
the different applications,

00:25:09.279 --> 00:25:11.570
different environments that
can connect into the shared

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:14.960
services and still have
separation of concerns

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:18.590
out in the spokes, where you're
connecting between the hub

00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:22.310
and spoke over a VPN, or if
you're doing a network peering.

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:25.580
Google has also introduced what
we call shared VPC networks,

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:29.420
where you have a single VPC,
a single network that is, say,

00:25:29.420 --> 00:25:32.420
administered by the network
administration group.

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:34.340
And other projects
have permission

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:38.030
to launch resources within
specific subnets in that group,

00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:40.080
inside that network.

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:43.025
And so this is a different way
of doing shared services that

00:25:43.025 --> 00:25:44.025
doesn't involve peering.

00:25:44.025 --> 00:25:46.131
It doesn't involve VPNs--

00:25:46.131 --> 00:25:47.630
if you need to have
shared services,

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:50.190
if you need to have still
separation of concern

00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:53.660
and separation of ownership
of resources, but you want

00:25:53.660 --> 00:25:56.040
applications still deployed
within the same network.

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:58.580
And so we've seen customers
have a lot of success with this.

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:04.650
And so that's the end
of my recommendations.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:09.330
That's the end of the five
steps of the cloud foundation.

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:12.990
Again, these are built out over
time and experience of what

00:26:12.990 --> 00:26:15.090
we have done with customers.

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:18.630
But we strongly recommend
that really building out

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:21.270
that foundation is going to
lead you to so much more success

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:24.120
and actually have a
better chance of moving

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:26.790
your migrations
forward more quickly

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:29.776
by doing this, for lack of a
better term, homework up front.

00:26:29.776 --> 00:26:32.400
If you've really built that out,
if you take the time to invest

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:34.419
in what this needs
to be, then you're

00:26:34.419 --> 00:26:36.210
going to have a great
deal of more success.

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:40.190
So thank you.

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:41.900
And we're going
to have questions

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:44.150
in just a few minutes,
so please stick around.

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:19.970
Hello.

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:20.990
Welcome back.

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:22.730
Thank you for these questions.

00:30:22.730 --> 00:30:24.650
I'm sure there are plenty more.

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:28.010
What we'll try to do afterwards
is get to those questions

00:30:28.010 --> 00:30:29.420
and answer them offline.

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:32.210
But we've selected
six here that seem

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:34.960
to touch on a few similar
concepts and questions.

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:38.930
So I'm going to go through
those and hopefully answer

00:30:38.930 --> 00:30:40.547
what you've asked me here.

00:30:40.547 --> 00:30:42.380
This first question--
what open source tools

00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:44.330
can help with automation?

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:45.750
There are a lot.

00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:49.910
And it really depends on
what point in your deployment

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:51.540
pipeline you're talking about.

00:30:51.540 --> 00:30:53.879
But you have everything
from right at the beginning

00:30:53.879 --> 00:30:54.920
to right at the very end.

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:56.545
So I'm going to run
through just a few.

00:30:56.545 --> 00:31:03.080
So if you look at
software that helps you

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:06.397
with your continuous
integration side of things,

00:31:06.397 --> 00:31:08.480
you've got a lot of things
that plug into Jenkins.

00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:11.060
So Jenkins is really
a fantastic controller

00:31:11.060 --> 00:31:12.950
for that side of
the pipeline where

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:18.440
you're integrating different
points of deployments; code

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:23.210
that's committed to Git or
wherever you keep your code;

00:31:23.210 --> 00:31:25.160
and being able to
package that up,

00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:28.010
being able to run integration
tests, unit tests, whatever

00:31:28.010 --> 00:31:30.610
those are, and then
deploying that out as well.

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:36.950
And integrating along
that side from Jenkins

00:31:36.950 --> 00:31:40.700
into something like, say, using
configuration tools, like Chef,

00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:43.940
and Puppet, and so on, really
actually make it fairly

00:31:43.940 --> 00:31:46.340
easy to have a situation where
you have a developer that

00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:49.280
can commit to a shared
repository for whatever

00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:50.480
project.

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:53.000
Jenkins knows that new
things have come in,

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:58.865
can run integration tests, and
then deploy that via, say, Chef

00:31:58.865 --> 00:32:01.580
or whatever you would like to
use, and be able to deploy that

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:02.600
onto virtual machines.

00:32:02.600 --> 00:32:05.540
And you can integrate
all that with, say,

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:08.630
a cloud native
environment configuration

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:11.840
tool like Deployment Manager
or an open source equivalent,

00:32:11.840 --> 00:32:14.780
something like
Terraform, where you've

00:32:14.780 --> 00:32:17.600
got Terraform or
Deployment Manager managing

00:32:17.600 --> 00:32:21.560
the actual resources and
something like Chef and Puppet

00:32:21.560 --> 00:32:23.600
managing the configuration
of your applications

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:25.700
on those resources.

00:32:25.700 --> 00:32:29.810
If you want to use
containers, then certainly

00:32:29.810 --> 00:32:33.370
things like Kubernetes,
which is open source--

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:36.530
it was created at Google, but
it is definitely open source--

00:32:36.530 --> 00:32:38.930
is a great container
management orchestration tool.

00:32:38.930 --> 00:32:41.190
It seems to be fairly
popular these days.

00:32:41.190 --> 00:32:43.970
And so if you are
using containers,

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:47.030
then you want to have a
container orchestration

00:32:47.030 --> 00:32:49.660
to also manage that.

00:32:49.660 --> 00:32:51.580
Second question--
how to encourage

00:32:51.580 --> 00:32:53.770
a culture of automation
to accelerate migration?

00:32:53.770 --> 00:32:56.390
That's actually a
fantastic question.

00:32:56.390 --> 00:32:59.140
I actually firmly believe that
automation is one of the best

00:32:59.140 --> 00:33:01.510
ways to accelerate migration.

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:03.010
And so this is really important.

00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:04.540
I think one of the
things to do is

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:08.560
to sell IT and
development on the idea

00:33:08.560 --> 00:33:11.740
that automation doesn't
just help migration.

00:33:11.740 --> 00:33:15.400
It helps developers, no matter
where they're deploying to.

00:33:15.400 --> 00:33:16.210
It helps IT.

00:33:16.210 --> 00:33:17.650
It helps operations.

00:33:17.650 --> 00:33:20.200
It helps security
because you can basically

00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:22.480
take all these steps
that are potentially

00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:24.770
manual at this point
and codify them.

00:33:24.770 --> 00:33:26.860
You can put them into
code and commit them.

00:33:26.860 --> 00:33:27.800
You can audit them.

00:33:27.800 --> 00:33:28.675
You can look at them.

00:33:28.675 --> 00:33:31.570
You can see what the processes
are, what the procedures are,

00:33:31.570 --> 00:33:34.720
to be able to improve them, to
be able to see what is actually

00:33:34.720 --> 00:33:35.560
done.

00:33:35.560 --> 00:33:37.630
And you're taking all
this operational overhead

00:33:37.630 --> 00:33:39.800
off of people who are
doing this manually.

00:33:39.800 --> 00:33:42.699
And so there are huge benefits
well beyond migration.

00:33:42.699 --> 00:33:44.740
And one of the other nice
things about automation

00:33:44.740 --> 00:33:47.380
is actually it's not an
all or nothing proposition.

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:51.100
You don't go from a completely
manually deployed pipeline

00:33:51.100 --> 00:33:53.640
to a fully automated
one in one step.

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:56.330
There's baby steps you can
take all along the way.

00:33:56.330 --> 00:33:58.690
And these incremental baby
steps have huge benefits

00:33:58.690 --> 00:34:00.050
at every single step.

00:34:00.050 --> 00:34:03.730
If all you want to do is move
to automated configuration

00:34:03.730 --> 00:34:07.090
management with
Ansible, then do that

00:34:07.090 --> 00:34:08.650
and you will see huge benefits.

00:34:08.650 --> 00:34:11.050
Even if you're not
fully automated

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:12.592
further up the
pipeline, you're still

00:34:12.592 --> 00:34:13.841
going to get a lot of benefit.

00:34:13.841 --> 00:34:15.340
Or from the other
side, if you just

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:17.949
want to implement Jenkins
so that every time you

00:34:17.949 --> 00:34:19.960
commit to your
repository you're running

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:22.679
some tests, that has huge
benefits right there already.

00:34:22.679 --> 00:34:25.270
And so I think the two
pronged approach towards going

00:34:25.270 --> 00:34:27.147
to a culture of
automation is one,

00:34:27.147 --> 00:34:29.230
everyone needs to understand
that this is not just

00:34:29.230 --> 00:34:29.860
for migration.

00:34:29.860 --> 00:34:32.690
It benefits day to day
operations of all of IT.

00:34:32.690 --> 00:34:35.014
And two, baby steps
are hugely beneficial.

00:34:35.014 --> 00:34:36.639
And you don't have
to do all or nothing

00:34:36.639 --> 00:34:40.989
to be able to really
benefit from automation.

00:34:40.989 --> 00:34:41.840
Third question.

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:45.341
What is the best way to get
started with a migration?

00:34:45.341 --> 00:34:46.840
I think you've got
to have a reason.

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:48.730
So why do you want to move?

00:34:48.730 --> 00:34:53.560
If you're in a data center,
are you capacity constrained?

00:34:53.560 --> 00:34:56.679
Are you not happy with
your current equipment?

00:34:56.679 --> 00:34:59.250
Is upgrading the equipment
going to be too expensive?

00:34:59.250 --> 00:35:01.780
Are you at the end of a lease?

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:04.450
Is there a reason why wherever
you are currently deployed

00:35:04.450 --> 00:35:05.170
is not working?

00:35:05.170 --> 00:35:07.379
Because that's often a
great motivating factor.

00:35:07.379 --> 00:35:08.920
On the other hand,
is there something

00:35:08.920 --> 00:35:12.370
in particular about moving
to Google Cloud that

00:35:12.370 --> 00:35:13.540
is particularly enticing?

00:35:13.540 --> 00:35:15.340
Is there something
very interesting

00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:19.609
about machine learning or using
Kubernetes on Google Cloud that

00:35:19.609 --> 00:35:20.650
is particularly enticing?

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:24.670
So I think getting a good reason
for why you want to migrate

00:35:24.670 --> 00:35:25.840
is absolutely important.

00:35:25.840 --> 00:35:27.474
And so once you
have that reason,

00:35:27.474 --> 00:35:29.890
this is the thing that overcomes
those business challenges

00:35:29.890 --> 00:35:31.306
that I was talking
about earlier--

00:35:31.306 --> 00:35:32.890
About motivating
people and getting

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:34.930
people convinced
that this is the best

00:35:34.930 --> 00:35:37.620
thing to do for your company
is the best way to get started.

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:40.150
So really finding that
motivating reason,

00:35:40.150 --> 00:35:42.214
and getting out there,
and pushing people

00:35:42.214 --> 00:35:43.630
to be able to start
that migration

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:46.850
is a good way to start.

00:35:46.850 --> 00:35:48.050
Next question.

00:35:48.050 --> 00:35:50.450
What resources are available
from Google and Google's

00:35:50.450 --> 00:35:53.490
partners to assist
with our migration?

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:55.580
So I'll speak about us first.

00:35:55.580 --> 00:36:00.260
We have tools that
are available,

00:36:00.260 --> 00:36:05.270
things like data transfer tools.

00:36:05.270 --> 00:36:09.380
So you can send us data offline.

00:36:09.380 --> 00:36:12.770
You can use data transfer
tools to transfer data

00:36:12.770 --> 00:36:16.610
from wherever it sits
into Google Cloud storage.

00:36:16.610 --> 00:36:20.510
You can work with
us on VM migration

00:36:20.510 --> 00:36:22.160
where we have a
VM migration tool.

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:24.200
We also have, like
I said, programs.

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:28.070
So things like, if you work with
our professional services team,

00:36:28.070 --> 00:36:29.710
they have several
migration programs

00:36:29.710 --> 00:36:31.380
that can help you move forward.

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:34.890
And so those are the
defined specifically

00:36:34.890 --> 00:36:38.010
for migration tools that
you can use from us.

00:36:38.010 --> 00:36:40.740
In addition to that, working
with Google account teams

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:44.250
and so on will certainly
enable those migrations.

00:36:44.250 --> 00:36:45.795
They'll help you
out in whatever way

00:36:45.795 --> 00:36:47.850
and connect you into the
various different groups

00:36:47.850 --> 00:36:49.685
at Google that can
help you with that.

00:36:49.685 --> 00:36:51.060
In terms of our
partners, we have

00:36:51.060 --> 00:36:55.500
a lot of technical partners,
like CloudEndure or Velostrata,

00:36:55.500 --> 00:37:01.290
that are all geared towards
automating migrations

00:37:01.290 --> 00:37:02.820
and migration tooling.

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:05.497
Attunity works on
data migration.

00:37:05.497 --> 00:37:06.580
So we have a few of those.

00:37:06.580 --> 00:37:10.260
We work with large partners
that will come in and assist you

00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:13.230
in terms of human resources.

00:37:13.230 --> 00:37:15.402
Moving your applications,
moving your data.

00:37:15.402 --> 00:37:16.860
And we partner with
a lot of those.

00:37:16.860 --> 00:37:20.640
And so you can go everywhere
from looking at our resources

00:37:20.640 --> 00:37:23.520
online, and reading
up documentation,

00:37:23.520 --> 00:37:25.840
to implementing our
tools, working with us,

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:28.720
working with partners, or any
kind of combination thereof.

00:37:28.720 --> 00:37:30.090
We do that fairly frequently.

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:32.490
I've done that many, many
times myself-- everything

00:37:32.490 --> 00:37:36.000
from only working with Googlers,
to only working with partners,

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:40.120
and, like I said,
everything in between.

00:37:40.120 --> 00:37:41.600
Question five.

00:37:41.600 --> 00:37:43.620
Does the shared
VPC project apply

00:37:43.620 --> 00:37:45.400
to both public and
private subnets?

00:37:45.400 --> 00:37:47.170
For private subnets,
what's the model

00:37:47.170 --> 00:37:50.110
for accessing the resource?

00:37:50.110 --> 00:37:52.510
So the first question, yeah.

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:56.680
In a shared VPC, you can make
public and private subnets.

00:37:56.680 --> 00:37:59.691
It's really very much dependent
on what sort of shared VPC

00:37:59.691 --> 00:38:00.190
you want.

00:38:00.190 --> 00:38:03.570
It's under the control of the
admin for that shared VPC.

00:38:03.570 --> 00:38:05.570
If you want all of the
subnets it to be private,

00:38:05.570 --> 00:38:06.236
you can do that.

00:38:06.236 --> 00:38:10.450
If you what none of them, some
sort of mix, you can do that.

00:38:10.450 --> 00:38:12.070
For private subnets,
what's the model

00:38:12.070 --> 00:38:14.680
for accessing the resource?

00:38:14.680 --> 00:38:16.600
I'm not entirely sure
I get the question,

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:19.120
but I'll try to answer.

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:22.960
The way shared VPCs work is
that there is a project that

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:26.860
owns the VPC, the shared VPC,
and it gives permission out

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:31.030
to other projects to launch
resources within that VPC,

00:38:31.030 --> 00:38:32.350
within a subnet.

00:38:32.350 --> 00:38:34.960
And so if you've decided
that this subnet is going

00:38:34.960 --> 00:38:38.380
to be a private subnet and
that project A has permission

00:38:38.380 --> 00:38:43.020
to launch resources, launch
VMs within that subnet,

00:38:43.020 --> 00:38:45.950
it's going to be able to
have total control over that.

00:38:45.950 --> 00:38:49.330
And then the network admin, so
the admin of the shared VPC,

00:38:49.330 --> 00:38:52.104
is going to determine how the
different subnets are allowed

00:38:52.104 --> 00:38:53.270
to interact with each other.

00:38:53.270 --> 00:38:55.394
Whether they're going to
be totally firewalled off,

00:38:55.394 --> 00:38:57.966
whether they're going to have
some access between each other,

00:38:57.966 --> 00:38:59.590
or if it's going to
be completely open,

00:38:59.590 --> 00:39:03.640
it's going to be determined
by that network admin.

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:06.320
And then finally, I guess
this is kind of related,

00:39:06.320 --> 00:39:08.660
please clarify the shared
VPC networks so you

00:39:08.660 --> 00:39:09.980
can touch on that once more.

00:39:09.980 --> 00:39:14.671
So, like I said, a
shared VPC is a VPC

00:39:14.671 --> 00:39:15.920
owned by a particular project.

00:39:15.920 --> 00:39:19.130
It's administered by
a particular project.

00:39:19.130 --> 00:39:21.320
The idea being though
that project is not going

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:23.540
to launch any resources itself.

00:39:23.540 --> 00:39:29.190
It is a way to collect
network administration

00:39:29.190 --> 00:39:31.790
within a single project
and be able to distribute

00:39:31.790 --> 00:39:34.720
that network out to
different projects

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:36.240
that it can then
launch resources.

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:38.990
And so the scenario
that this is built for,

00:39:38.990 --> 00:39:41.930
and we've seen this with
quite a few customers,

00:39:41.930 --> 00:39:46.340
is that network
administrators need to control

00:39:46.340 --> 00:39:47.690
what happens in the network.

00:39:47.690 --> 00:39:51.380
But development teams
are relatively autonomous

00:39:51.380 --> 00:39:54.390
and own the resources
that they deploy.

00:40:05.835 --> 00:40:10.290
And so when the development
team deploys resources,

00:40:10.290 --> 00:40:12.810
they want to be able to own
that, and be able to control

00:40:12.810 --> 00:40:15.420
that, and be able to say,
this is what we want to do,

00:40:15.420 --> 00:40:18.790
and this is not what we want
to do, with our resources.

00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:22.380
But also we don't want to
administer our own network.

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:25.480
We want the network admin team
to be able to handle that.

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:27.780
We just want to be able to
control our own resources.

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:33.520
And so that is kind of the goal
and the idealized situation

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:35.430
of what a shared
VPC is used for.

00:40:35.430 --> 00:40:38.430
There are actually a lot
more use cases for that.

00:40:38.430 --> 00:40:39.950
In particular, for
shared services

00:40:39.950 --> 00:40:41.400
is where it comes up a lot.

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:43.380
But the case being
most of the time

00:40:43.380 --> 00:40:48.450
it's when teams own the
resources, when they

00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:51.030
own the applications, when
they own the services that

00:40:51.030 --> 00:40:53.910
are deployed, but do not
want to or should not

00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:55.740
own the actual network
administration,

00:40:55.740 --> 00:40:58.440
is really what it's geared for.

00:40:58.440 --> 00:41:01.460
And that is it
for our questions.

00:41:01.460 --> 00:41:02.619
And so thank you very much.

00:41:02.619 --> 00:41:03.660
Thank you for joining me.

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:06.270
And the next session
is coming up shortly.

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:08.180
Thank you very much.

